ia: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention/accuracy, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability. For B: reliability (conscientious), stress (handles well), technical (weather, computer), decision quality unknown, attention/accuracy (weak memory for numbers), crew cooperation (nasty remarks), communication (nasty remarks), responsibility unknown, adaptability unknown. For C: reliability (100% reliable), stress (keeps calm), technical (understands complicated technology), decision quality (quick correct decisions), attention/accuracy (not mentioned but likely okay), crew cooperation (creates positive atmosphere), communication (difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric), responsibility (not mentioned), adaptability (not willing to further education). So C has many strengths, but adaptability concerns.\n\nGiven the panel hasn't discussed adaptability for C, but we have note \"not very willing to further his education\" which suggests low adaptability. That could be a serious issue for long-distance flights where continuous learning is needed. B also has unknown adaptability but no negative note. B's numeric memory weakness could affect attention/accuracy.\n\nWe could argue that both have concerns, but B's communication issues are more severe (nasty remarks) and may affect safety. C's egocentric may be mitigated by positive atmosphere. So we could shift vote to C.\n\nThus as Agent 2, we can change recommendation to Candidate C, providing reasoning: C's reliability, calmness, decision quality, positive crew atmosphere outweigh B's communication issues and numeric memory weakness. Also note C's difficulty communicating and egocentric but positive atmosphere suggests crew cooperation is okay. Also note C's unwillingness to further education is a concern but may be less critical than B's nasty remarks.\n\nWe need to reference discussion: Agent 1 and 3 supporting B, but we raise concerns about B's co